A quantum kernel compares data through the fidelity of an encoding, so the geometry
  the model can express is fixed before any training happens. Hierarchical data
  (taxonomies, ontologies, phylogenies) is not Euclidean but ultrametric, and we ask
  which quantum feature maps induce an exactly ultrametric kernel. We give two
  obstructions and a matching construction. First, no product feature map does, whatever
  its local dimensions: multiplicativity across digits limits any such encoding to a
  bounded resolution depth, which covers angle and computational-basis encodings.
  Second, and independently, the Gram matrix of any exact realisation forces a
  dimension of at least $L^{2} / \sum_{x,y} K(x,y)$ on $L$ points; on the $p$-adic tree
  with a geometrically decaying profile this exceeds $2^{n}$ for every $p \ge 3$, so no
  $n$-qubit encoding suffices, including the entangling IQP and ZZ maps the first
  argument cannot reach. Third, \emph{path states}, superpositions over a leaf's
  root-to-leaf ancestor chain with amplitudes read off the target profile, realise
  precisely the strictly monotone ultrametric kernels, on arbitrary rooted trees, using
  a number of qubits within an additive constant of that lower bound. We further show
  that global depolarising noise cannot break ultrametricity at any rate, since it acts
  on distances as an increasing affine map, and we measure the shot budget that finite
  sampling demands. Experiments on WordNet, Gene Ontology and the NCBI taxonomy confirm
  exactness to machine precision and separate ultrametricity from usable resolution: a
  basis encoding is ultrametric while resolving nothing. We claim no quantum speedup in
  kernel evaluation, and say so throughout; the contribution is a constraint on
  encoding design and an optimal-width encoding for models where no closed form
  survives.
